Relevant tables / columns, how they will be used and typical conditions  
• olist_orders.order_id : primary key to connect orders with payments and customers  
• olist_orders.customer_id : foreign key → olist_customers; brings the city into the query  
• olist_orders.order_status : must equal 'delivered' to meet the problem statement  
• olist_customers.customer_id : join field coming from olist_orders  
• olist_customers.customer_city : city that we finally aggregate by  
• olist_order_payments.order_id : join field coming from olist_orders  
• olist_order_payments.payment_value : numeric value to be summed per order / city  

Typical WHERE clause(s):  
• olist_orders.order_status = 'delivered'  → keep only delivered orders  


Ten exploratory‑to‑solution queries  
(Every query is limited to 20 rows for inspection and uses DISTINCT whenever it makes sense.)

```sql
-- 1️⃣ Description: Inspect the different order statuses that exist.
SELECT DISTINCT "order_status"
FROM "olist_orders"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2️⃣ Description: Show 20 delivered orders so we know how an order_id looks.
SELECT "order_id", "customer_id", "order_status"
FROM "olist_orders"
WHERE "order_status" = 'delivered'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3️⃣ Description: Quick look at the distinct customer cities.
SELECT DISTINCT "customer_city"
FROM "olist_customers"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4️⃣ Description: Verify that each customer_id can give us its city.
SELECT "customer_id", "customer_city"
FROM "olist_customers"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5️⃣ Description: Join delivered orders with customers to see what cities are attached.
SELECT o."order_id",
       c."customer_city"
FROM   "olist_orders"  AS o
JOIN   "olist_customers" AS c
       ON c."customer_id" = o."customer_id"
WHERE  o."order_status" = 'delivered'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6️⃣ Description: Peek at raw payment records to understand payment_value.
SELECT "order_id",
       "payment_sequential",
       "payment_value"
FROM   "olist_order_payments"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7️⃣ Description: Sum payments per order (all payment rows collapsed).
SELECT "order_id",
       SUM("payment_value") AS "order_payment"
FROM   "olist_order_payments"
GROUP  BY "order_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8️⃣ Description: Attach the summed‑per‑order payments to each delivered order and city.
SELECT o."order_id",
       c."customer_city",
       SUM(p."payment_value") AS "order_payment"
FROM   "olist_orders"  AS o
JOIN   "olist_order_payments" AS p  ON p."order_id"  = o."order_id"
JOIN   "olist_customers"      AS c  ON c."customer_id" = o."customer_id"
WHERE  o."order_status" = 'delivered'
GROUP  BY o."order_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9️⃣ Description: Aggregate to city level – total payments and delivered‑order counts,
--                 then order by the summed payments (ascending).
SELECT d."customer_city"                           AS "city",
       SUM(d."order_payment")                      AS "total_payments",
       COUNT(*)                                    AS "delivered_orders"
FROM  (
        SELECT o."order_id",
               c."customer_city",
               SUM(p."payment_value") AS "order_payment"
        FROM   "olist_orders"  AS o
        JOIN   "olist_order_payments" AS p ON p."order_id"  = o."order_id"
        JOIN   "olist_customers"      AS c ON c."customer_id" = o."customer_id"
        WHERE  o."order_status" = 'delivered'
        GROUP  BY o."order_id"
      ) AS d
GROUP BY d."customer_city"
ORDER BY "total_payments" ASC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 🔟 Description (final answer query): 
-- 1. Re‑use the city‑level aggregation from step 9.  
-- 2. Keep only the five cities with the lowest total payments.  
-- 3. Compute the averages requested.
WITH delivered_orders AS (
    SELECT o."order_id",
           c."customer_city",
           SUM(p."payment_value") AS "order_payment"
    FROM   "olist_orders"          AS o
    JOIN   "olist_order_payments"  AS p ON p."order_id"  = o."order_id"
    JOIN   "olist_customers"       AS c ON c."customer_id" = o."customer_id"
    WHERE  o."order_status" = 'delivered'
    GROUP  BY o."order_id"
),
city_agg AS (
    SELECT "customer_city"                          AS "city",
           SUM("order_payment")                     AS "total_payments",
           COUNT(*)                                 AS "delivered_orders"
    FROM   delivered_orders
    GROUP  BY "customer_city"
),
five_lowest AS (
    SELECT *
    FROM   city_agg
    ORDER  BY "total_payments" ASC
    LIMIT 5
)
SELECT AVG("total_payments")   AS "avg_of_city_total_payments",
       AVG("delivered_orders") AS "avg_of_city_delivered_orders"
FROM   five_lowest;
```
